{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfShV1xi1uAx5R1h8CxQC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 14 is done, the loop is complete, and nothing is left running. The only process `pgrep` matched was the polling shell itself, as earlier iterations warned.\n\n**What the session did.** No item was blocked, so it took the first \"for the next loop\" note (§21's: exhaust the 9-vertex graphs so the 10-vertex `pw − tw = 2` record becomes a theorem) and went two sizes further, because the estimate in §21 was off by three orders of magnitude in the safe direction: the subset DPs cost microseconds in C, not the Python DP's seconds.\n\n**Findings**, written as §27 of `reports/ml_nature.md` with tables in `reports/pwtw_tables.md`:\n- **Theorem.** Every graph on at most 9 vertices has `pw ≤ tw + 1`. All 287,884 isomorphism classes were enumerated with nauty's `geng` and both widths computed exactly. §21's 10-vertex graph is therefore a smallest graph with `pw − tw = 2`.\n- **The four graphs on 10 vertices** with the gap are §21's graph with its tenth vertex joined to any subset of the three hubs, a chain of 21 to 24 edges. The 21-edge graph is edge-minimal and the 24-edge one edge-maximal.\n- **On 11 vertices** there are 1,034 such graphs and none with a gap of 3, so a gap of 3 needs at least 12 vertices. 516 are new vertex-minimal examples. Twelve have treewidth 2 and pathwidth 4 with no K4 at all. The sparsest, 15 edges and maximum degree 3, is three triangles strung between two poles, biconnected and not a tree of cliques, which makes it a second shape for any future bound work.\n- The share of graphs with pathwidth strictly above treewidth rises from 0.6% at n = 6 to 9.9% at n = 11.\n\n**Verification.** Every count matches OEIS A000088, the treewidth-1 column matches the number of forests (A005195) at every n, the C agrees with the Python references on 1,552 graphs, and all 1,038 graphs found were re-established by independent routes including the exact MOSP solver with persisted witnesses, both `decide` configurations, and the lattice oracle. All 1,038 agree.\n\n**Deliverables.** `learning/pwtw_exhaust.c`, `learning/pwtw_exhaust.py`, `tests/test_pwtw_exhaust.py` (20 tests), three small committed CSVs and logs under the ensemble directory (82 MB apparent in total), a `.gitignore` update for the scratch outputs. Compute was 20 s for n ≤ 10 and 49 minutes on 16 workers for n = 11. The full suite passes with 1,048 tests. No solver default, bound or C path was touched and nothing was written to `solutions/`.\n\n**Bookkeeping.** PROGRESS.md carries the Iteration 16 section, reads 14/14 SOLVED, and ends with `LOOP_COMPLETE`. Item 14 is marked `- [x]` in iterations.md. Nothing was committed, per the driver's rule. The n = 12 census is priced in §27 at 4,300 to 4,700 core-hours for the full joint table or about 240 for the gap question alone, and left for a future loop.\n\nSources consulted for prior art: [Approximating pathwidth for graphs of small treewidth (arXiv)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.00779), [OEIS A000088](https://oeis.org/A000088), [OEIS A005418](https://oeis.org/A005418), [Pathwidth (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathwidth), [Treewidth (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treewidth). None reports a census of small graphs, so no novelty is claimed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":875,"cache_read_input_tokens":222281,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":875,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":777,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1c5ce58-56d5-4562-84ae-5b3ca08c38a5","uuid":"6954bdc4-f1ae-494a-afdf-df60aa85d68a","timestamp":"2026-09-26T20:41:27.714Z","request_id":"req_011CfShV139R2B8esGHi1agM"}
